e use for you know providers over  a wide variety of settings including at  home where observation really isn't it  not possibility so we were able to do  this in ophthalmology so this is total  EHR use both in clinic and out of clinic  and we actually categorize it as well  whether it was a way that's directly  related to patient visit versus other  things like inbox measuring and that  sort of thing  and this just sort of shows that that  providers are consistently using the HR  really at least half of their day some  of them even more and we think this is  an underestimate  so this EHR is really predominating in  their work and not only that but we were  able to see over time how this has  changed so at OHSU we started using an  EHR in 2006 so we looked at 10 years of  use  you can see that it increased and we  were able to actually sort of corley  that and I shouldn't use a Recor like  that line that up with when meaningful  use the different forms of meaningful  use happened and you can see that there  was a big bump in the amount of time  that providers were using the HR for the  different meaningful use stages but it  looks like it's kind of stabilizing and  we're studying that right now in terms  of what how people are using it and why  you know it might be becoming more  efficient or maybe leveling off and  finally the one of the ways we've used  this to measure this documentation time  is we were able to use and see what the  impact of scribes is on provider  documentation time so we were able to  show that in the patient appointment  obviously providers aren't using the EHR  as much because subscribe is using the  EHR but this graph shows you what  happens after the visit and you can pour  seven of our ophthalmology providers  four of them actually spent more time on  average using the EHR after the visit  then when they didn't have a scribe and  three of them actually used it less but  this is an important kind of an  important result because one of the 